;; Docs for std/test/expect.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "expect.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Snapshot tests: expect-output compares what a test wrote with a text in the test, and --promote updates the text.")
  (see expect-output deftest)
  (example """"
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std test))
    (import (std test expect))

    (deftest "printing a card"
      (print-value (Card (rank 12) (suit Hearts)))
      (expect-output """
        (Card (rank 12) (suit Hearts))
        """))

    (run-tests)
    """")
  (reference """
    @code{expect-output} compares the output of a test with a text that is
    written in the test. When they differ, the test fails, and the report
    shows a line diff. With @code{--promote}, the run writes the output into
    the test file in place of the text, and the test does not fail.

    @strong{Which tests capture their output.} @code{deftest} examines its
    body. When the body has the name @code{expect-output} anywhere in it,
    the output of the test goes to a string port, and
    @code{current-output-port} does not print it. A function that is
    defined outside the test and calls @code{expect-output} does not make
    the test capture its output. Then @code{expect-output} has no output to
    read, and the test fails.

    @strong{What is compared.} Each @code{expect-output} reads what the test
    wrote since the test started, or since the previous
    @code{expect-output}. Both texts are split into lines. Whitespace at the
    end of each line is removed, and empty lines at the start and the end of
    the text are removed. So a final newline, or a carriage return, is not a
    difference.

    @strong{The diff.} A line that only the expected text has starts with
    -, a line that only the output has starts with +, and a line that both
    have starts with a space:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    FAIL printing two cards (cards_test.bjo:10)
      cards_test.bjo:13: the output is not the expected text (- expected, + written; --promote updates the text):
         (Card (rank 12) (suit Hearts))
        -(Card (rank 1) (suit Spades))
        +(Card (rank 1) (suit Clubs))
    }|

    The longest common subsequence of the lines decides which lines both
    texts have. When the product of the two numbers of lines is more than
    a million, the diff shows all the expected lines with - and all the
    written lines with +.

    @strong{Promotion.} With @code{--promote}, an @code{expect-output}
    whose text differs records the output, and the test does not fail. At
    the end of @code{(run-tests)}, through @code{at-run-end!}, the run
    writes the output into the test file in place of each text that
    differed, and prints a line for each file that it changed. The text
    must be a string literal that comes directly after
    @code{expect-output}, because the run looks for it there. The run
    prints a line for each text that it could not find.

    The new text is a raw string. Its lines start 2 columns to the right of
    the column of the @code{(expect-output}, and the closing quotes are on a
    line of their own, in the same column. A raw string has more quotes
    than the longest run of quotes in the text, and at least three. Output
    that has no lines becomes @code{""}.

    The file is changed from its last line to its first, so that a change
    does not move the lines of the texts that come before it. When one
    @code{expect-output} runs more than once in a run, for example in a
    @code{for-all}, the output of its first mismatch is written.
    @code{run-test} alone does not write the file.

    @strong{Output that no expect-output reads.} What the test wrote after
    its last @code{expect-output} is printed when the test ends, so that the
    prints of a test are not lost.
    """))

(:doc expect-output
  (summary "Passes when what the test wrote since it started, or since the previous expect-output, is the text.")
  (form (expect-output text))
  (arg text "A string: the expected output. Write it as a string literal, so that @code{--promote} can replace it.")
  (see deftest check-output)
  (example """"
    (deftest "a greeting"
      (println "hello")
      (println "world")
      (expect-output """
        hello
        world
        """)
      (println "again")
      (expect-output "again"))

    (deftest "prints nothing"
      (expect-output ""))
    """")
  (reference """
    Write it in the body of a @code{deftest}, not in a function that the
    body calls: @code{deftest} captures the output of the test only when its
    body has the name @code{expect-output}.

    Lines are compared without the whitespace at their ends, and without
    the empty lines at the start and the end of the text. On a mismatch,
    the test fails with a line diff. With @code{--promote}, the output is
    written into the test file in place of the text when the run ends, and
    the test does not fail. The module tells more.

    For the output of one expression, @code{check-output} from
    @code{(std test)} is shorter. It captures the output also in a test
    whose body does not have the name @code{expect-output}.
    """))

(:doc expect-output-check!
  (summary "Compares the output of the running test with a text, and fails the test or records a promotion.")
  (arg file "The source file of the expect-output.")
  (arg line "The line of the expect-output.")
  (arg wanted "The expected text.")
  (see expect-output)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{expect-output} names it.
    Write @code{expect-output}, not a call to this function.

    When the running test does not capture its output, it fails the test
    with a message that says so.
    """))
